      <h2>Trajectory</h2>
      <p>
        I'm <strong>Tianle Chen</strong> (陈天乐), also known as <strong>David</strong>. I'm a Master
        of Science in Computational Design student at Carnegie Mellon University,
        finishing a thesis on AI-augmented footwear design. The research artifact is
        <a href="/work/semantic-canvas">Semantic Canvas</a>, an interactive
        latent-space tool deployed for multi-user design studies.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before CMU, I studied architecture at Rice University (Bachelor of Architecture,
        Class of 2024), where I worked with <strong>Prof. Juan Jose Castellon</strong> as
        a computational research assistant. Our research on segmental ceramic hollow
        columns was published at the
        <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/384055100_Segmental_Ceramic_Hollow_Structures_Prefabricated_posttensioned_columns_for_ecological_urban_infrastructures" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IASS 2024 Symposium</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've held architecture roles at <strong>EID Architect</strong>, <strong>Pure Architect</strong>,
        <strong>ECADI</strong> (Shanghai), and <strong>NBBJ</strong> (Los Angeles). Originally from
        Shanghai; currently in Pittsburgh with my wife and two cats.
      </p>

      <h2>What I work on</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Design tools + ML.</strong> Interactive systems where generative AI reshapes expert workflows — latent-space exploration, sketch-to-3D, real-time ambient projection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Generative 3D.</strong> Vision Transformer encoders, triplane representations, multimodal CAD generation, custom dataset pipelines.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Computational design.</strong> Parametric form-finding, robotic fabrication, topology optimization — inherited from my architecture training, now applied to ML research problems.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Interests</h2>
      <p>
        The central question: when a designer uses a generative AI tool, what shape
        should that collaboration take so design judgment stays intact and compounds
        over time? I'm drawn to work where the answer matters — design-tool companies,
        AI studios doing HCI/generative-UI research, architecture or design firms
        doing R&amp;D. Open to remote and to relocating.
      </p>

